Recognize touch gestures locally when the WebProcess is unresponsive
(WEBKIT_TOUCH_ACK_TIMEOUT_MS, default 100; =0 disables the timeout) and
stop dropping gesture events that arrive on the late-reply path.

During a heavy page load the WebProcess main thread is saturated for many
seconds. In the WPE port every raw touch event round-trips to the
WebProcess and the UIProcess TouchGestureController only sees it when the
reply comes back (PageClientImpl::doneWithTouchEvent); on top of that, the
axis (scroll) events generated on that reply path were discarded by an
empty visitor. Scrolling only ever worked through the raw-event short-cut
that requires the controller to ALREADY be in Axis state -- a state it can
only enter via a reply. Net effect, device-proven: a flick during page
load is not delayed, it is lost entirely (scrollY stays 0 even after the
load completes).

Two changes, both UIProcess-side:

1. doneWithTouchEvent now dispatches the generated axis events, guarded on
   the controller not already having been in Axis state when the reply was
   processed (in that case the raw short-cut already applied the delta and
   the reply-path event is a zero-delta duplicate; re-dispatching a late
   phase=Ended duplicate would kill an ongoing kinetic fling).

2. Chrome-style touch-ack timeout: if the oldest still-unacked touch event
   is older than WEBKIT_TOUCH_ACK_TIMEOUT_MS when the next raw event
   arrives, the sequence is gesture-recognized locally in the UIProcess
   (replaying the stored touch-down into the controller first), and its
   late replies are suppressed via a per-sequence generation table so the
   gesture is not applied twice. The synthesized wheel events reach the
   WebProcess EventDispatcher, which hands non-blocking scrolls to the
   scrolling thread -- so the page scrolls DURING load, without waiting for
   the busy main thread. Quick taps recognized locally synthesize the same
   click sequence the reply path would have.

Touch events are still forwarded to the page in all cases, so WebCore's
touch state stays consistent; a late preventDefault from the page is
ignored for locally-handled sequences (same trade-off as Chrome's touch
ack timeout).

